# Building a Reusable Windows Golden Image for Cybersecurity Labs

Creating individual Windows virtual machines for every lab user quickly becomes repetitive and difficult to maintain.

A better approach is to build one clean Windows system, prepare it for cloning, generalize it with Sysprep, and use it as the foundation for future lab workstations.

This guide walks through that process using Proxmox VE, Windows, Active Directory, and Wazuh.

---

# 1. What Are We Building?

The target architecture is:

```text
Windows Base VM
      |
      v
Configure Lab Software
      |
      v
Remove Machine-Specific Identity
      |
      v
Sysprep / Generalize
      |
      v
Golden Image
      |
      +----------+----------+
      |          |          |
      v          v          v
   Clone 01   Clone 02   Clone 03
      |          |          |
      v          v          v
Unique AD     Unique AD   Unique AD
Identity      Identity    Identity

Unique Wazuh  Unique Wazuh Unique Wazuh
Identity      Identity     Identity
```

The important concept is:

> Clone the desired operating system state, not the identity of the source computer.

---

# 2. Why Use a Golden Image?

Without a reusable image, every new workstation requires:

```text
Install Windows
      |
Install Updates
      |
Install Tools
      |
Configure Settings
      |
Install Monitoring
      |
Configure Remote Access
      |
Repeat Everything Again
```

A golden image moves the reusable work earlier in the process:

```text
Build Once
    |
Generalize
    |
Clone
    |
Assign Unique Identity
```

This becomes especially useful when building:

- Student workstations
- SOC analyst endpoints
- Help Desk labs
- Incident-response exercises
- Malware-analysis environments
- Temporary training systems

---

# 3. Understand What Cloning Copies

A VM clone can contain much more than the Windows installation.

Depending on when the source is cloned, it may contain:

- Computer name
- Local accounts
- Application configuration
- Domain-related state
- Monitoring agent configuration
- Authentication material
- Certificates
- Cached data
- Security tooling
- Log files

This means:

```text
VM Clone ≠ Automatically Unique Computer
```

The cloning workflow must deliberately separate reusable configuration from machine-specific identity.

---

# 4. Reference Technology

This guide assumes:

```text
Hypervisor:
Proxmox VE

Operating System:
Windows 10/11 Pro or Enterprise

Identity:
Microsoft Active Directory

Endpoint Monitoring:
Wazuh

Remote Access:
RDP
```

The concepts also apply to other hypervisors and monitoring platforms.

---

# 5. Build the Base Windows VM

Start with a clean Windows virtual machine.

Recommended baseline tasks include:

- Install Windows
- Install current updates
- Install required drivers
- Configure the virtual NIC
- Install required classroom or security tools
- Remove unnecessary temporary files
- Verify Windows activation where applicable
- Confirm the system is stable

Do not generalize a system that is already experiencing unresolved operating-system problems.

---

# 6. Create an Instructor Recovery Account

Before removing domain membership or changing machine identity, create a local administrative recovery account.

Example:

```text
LabAdmin
```

Verify that it belongs to the local Administrators group:

```powershell
net localgroup Administrators
```

Then verify that you can actually sign in using:

```text
.\LabAdmin
```

This provides a recovery path if domain authentication becomes unavailable.

Do not publish the password.

---

# 7. Why a Local Recovery Account Matters

Consider a workstation that currently depends on Active Directory:

```text
Windows VM
   |
   v
Domain Authentication
```

If you remove that system from the domain before verifying local administrative access, you can create an unnecessary recovery problem.

The safer sequence is:

```text
Create Local Administrator
        |
        v
Test Local Login
        |
        v
Remove Domain Identity
```

---

# 8. Decide What Belongs in the Image

A golden image should contain **reusable configuration**.

Good candidates include:

- Operating-system updates
- Common applications
- Endpoint monitoring software binaries
- Administrative utilities
- Classroom software
- Browser configuration
- General security settings

Avoid embedding machine-specific secrets such as:

- Enrollment keys
- API tokens
- Private keys
- User passwords
- Cloud credentials
- Temporary access tokens

Those should be assigned after deployment.

---

# 9. Domain-Joined Source Systems

If your source VM is already joined to Active Directory, do not simply clone it repeatedly and use the clones as independent domain computers.

The source has an existing relationship with Active Directory.

A reusable image should be prepared so deployed clones can establish their own computer identities.

---

# 10. Remove the Source from the Domain

Using the local administrator account, remove the preparation VM from the domain and return it to a workgroup.

One PowerShell method is:

```powershell
Remove-Computer `
  -UnjoinDomainCredential "<DOMAIN>\<AUTHORIZED_ACCOUNT>" `
  -WorkgroupName "WORKGROUP" `
  -Restart
```

Enter the credential interactively.

Do not hard-code domain credentials into the script.

---

# 11. Validate Domain Removal

After restart:

```powershell
Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem |
Select-Object Name,Domain,PartOfDomain
```

Expected:

```text
Domain       : WORKGROUP
PartOfDomain : False
```

Do not proceed based only on the absence of an error message.

Validate the resulting state.

---

# 12. Prepare Endpoint Monitoring

Security agents deserve special attention before cloning.

An installed monitoring agent may contain identity information used by the management platform.

For Wazuh, this can include:

```text
Agent Name
Agent ID
Enrollment State
client.keys
```

If every clone inherits the same identity, the SIEM may interpret multiple computers as the same endpoint or reject the duplicates.

---

# 13. Stop the Wazuh Agent

Before preparing the reusable image:

```powershell
Stop-Service WazuhSvc
```

Verify:

```powershell
Get-Service WazuhSvc
```

The goal is to prevent the preparation system from reconnecting while its identity is being cleaned.

---

# 14. Remove Machine-Specific Wazuh Identity

The Wazuh agent installation itself can remain available in the image.

The reusable enrollment identity should not.

A common Windows installation location is:

```text
C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\
```

If an existing `client.keys` file belongs to the source endpoint, it should not become the permanent identity used by every clone.

For example, preserve it temporarily while preparing the image:

```powershell
Rename-Item `
  "C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\client.keys" `
  "client.keys.pre-generalization"
```

Before publishing or distributing the image, make sure sensitive enrollment material is not exposed.

---

# 15. Why Monitoring Identity Must Be Unique

Consider this incorrect design:

```text
Source VM
Agent Identity 005
      |
      +---------+
      |         |
      v         v
Clone A      Clone B
ID 005       ID 005
```

The monitoring platform cannot reliably distinguish the systems.

The correct design is:

```text
Golden Image
      |
      +----------+
      |          |
      v          v
Clone A       Clone B
      |          |
      v          v
Agent A       Agent B
```

Each deployed endpoint receives its own monitoring identity.

---

# 16. Prepare for Sysprep

Before running Sysprep, verify:

```text
[ ] Local administrator tested
[ ] Domain membership removed if required
[ ] Required software installed
[ ] Updates completed
[ ] No installation is actively running
[ ] Machine-specific monitoring identity removed
[ ] Sensitive temporary files removed
[ ] System is stable
```

A snapshot before generalization can also provide a useful recovery point during image development.

---

# 17. What Does Sysprep Do?

Sysprep is Microsoft's System Preparation tool.

For this workflow, two options matter:

```text
OOBE
Generalize
```

### OOBE

OOBE causes the deployed clone to enter the Windows Out-of-Box Experience on first boot.

### Generalize

Generalization removes system-specific information so Windows can be deployed as a new machine.

Conceptually:

```text
Configured Windows System
          |
          v
       Sysprep
          |
          v
Generalized Windows Image
```

---

# 18. Run Sysprep

Launch:

```powershell
C:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\Sysprep.exe
```

Select:

```text
System Cleanup Action:
Enter System Out-of-Box Experience (OOBE)

Generalize:
Checked

Shutdown Options:
Shutdown
```

Start Sysprep and allow Windows to shut down automatically.

---

# 19. Do Not Interrupt Generalization

Once Sysprep begins:

- Do not force-stop the VM
- Do not reboot it manually
- Do not close the process
- Do not reset the VM

Wait for Windows to shut itself down.

---

# 20. Troubleshooting Sysprep Validation Errors

A common failure is:

```text
Sysprep was not able to validate your Windows installation.
```

Instead of repeatedly retrying Sysprep, inspect its logs.

The primary log directory is:

```text
C:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\Panther\
```

Review:

```powershell
Get-Content `
  "C:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\Panther\setuperr.log" `
  -Tail 40
```

The log usually provides a much more useful explanation than the graphical error message.

---

# 21. Common AppX Problem

One common failure pattern is:

```text
Package <PACKAGE> was installed for a user,
but not provisioned for all users.
```

This can happen when Microsoft Store/AppX applications change after Windows installation.

Do not respond by deleting every AppX package.

Identify the package named in the Sysprep log first.

---

# 22. Inspect Installed AppX Packages

Example:

```powershell
Get-AppxPackage -AllUsers |
Where-Object {$_.Name -like "*<PACKAGE>*"}
```

Inspect provisioned packages:

```powershell
Get-AppxProvisionedPackage -Online |
Where-Object {$_.DisplayName -like "*<PACKAGE>*"}
```

This helps determine whether the application is:

```text
Installed for a user
Provisioned for future users
Both
```

---

# 23. Remove Only the Blocking Package

If the Sysprep log identifies a specific package and you determine it is safe to remove:

```powershell
Get-AppxPackage -AllUsers *<PACKAGE>* |
Remove-AppxPackage -AllUsers
```

If a corresponding provisioned package must also be removed:

```powershell
Get-AppxProvisionedPackage -Online |
Where-Object {$_.DisplayName -like "*<PACKAGE>*"} |
Remove-AppxProvisionedPackage -Online
```

Then run Sysprep again.

---

# 24. Troubleshooting Lesson

During the MutaSpace build, multiple AppX packages had to be investigated before Sysprep completed successfully.

The important lesson was not the names of those specific applications.

The reusable troubleshooting method was:

```text
Sysprep fails
      |
      v
Read setuperr.log
      |
      v
Identify exact package
      |
      v
Inspect installation/provisioning state
      |
      v
Remove only the blocker
      |
      v
Retry
```

That workflow is more useful than blindly applying a list of package-removal commands.

---

# 25. Successful Generalization

When Sysprep succeeds, the VM should shut down.

At this point:

> Do not boot the generalized master just to check whether it worked.

Booting it begins the deployment/OOBE process.

Instead, preserve the powered-off VM as the source for future clones.

---

# 26. Protect the Golden Image

Treat the generalized master as infrastructure.

Recommended practices:

- Keep it powered off
- Give it a clear name
- Do not assign it to students
- Do not use it for ordinary testing
- Clone from it instead of booting it
- Document major image versions

Example:

```text
windows-lab-golden-v1
```

---

# 27. Create a Full Clone in Proxmox

From the generalized master:

```text
Clone
  |
  v
Full Clone
```

A full clone gives the new VM independent storage.

This is useful for student environments because each machine can be changed, broken, restored, or deleted independently.

---

# 28. Naming Strategy

Use predictable names.

Example:

```text
helpdesk-team01
helpdesk-team02
helpdesk-team03
```

or:

```text
student-win01
student-win02
student-win03
```

Consistent naming makes later work with:

- Active Directory
- Wazuh
- DNS
- Guacamole
- SIEM searches

much easier.

---

# 29. Interrupted Clone Operations

A clone operation should normally be allowed to finish.

If a clone is interrupted, Proxmox may leave the destination VM locked.

First determine whether a clone task is still running.

Do not unlock a VM while a legitimate operation is active.

If the task has stopped and the clone is incomplete:

```bash
qm unlock <VM-ID>
```

The incomplete clone can then be removed and recreated.

---

# 30. Complete OOBE

Start the new clone.

Because the image was generalized, Windows should enter OOBE.

Complete the initial setup according to your lab's account model.

For a local lab account, Windows Pro editions may provide:

```text
Set up for an organization
```

and:

```text
Domain join instead
```

The wording can vary between Windows releases.

---

# 31. Assign a Unique Computer Name

After deployment, assign the clone its permanent hostname.

Example:

```powershell
Rename-Computer -NewName "HELPDESK-TEAM01" -Restart
```

Every clone must have a unique name.

---

# 32. Validate Networking

Before attempting Active Directory enrollment, verify the endpoint's network configuration.

```powershell
ipconfig /all
```

Then test:

```powershell
Test-Connection <GATEWAY_IP> -Count 2
Test-Connection <DOMAIN_CONTROLLER_IP> -Count 2
```

Verify DNS:

```powershell
Resolve-DnsName <DOMAIN_CONTROLLER_FQDN>
```

---

# 33. Why DNS Comes Before Domain Join

Active Directory depends heavily on DNS.

A computer may have:

```text
Working Internet
```

while still being unable to join the domain because it is using the wrong DNS server.

The correct troubleshooting order is:

```text
IP Configuration
      |
      v
Gateway
      |
      v
Internal DNS
      |
      v
Domain Controller Resolution
      |
      v
Domain Join
```

---

# 34. Join the Clone to Active Directory

Once networking and DNS are validated:

```powershell
Add-Computer `
  -DomainName "<LAB_DOMAIN>" `
  -Credential "<AUTHORIZED_DOMAIN_ACCOUNT>" `
  -Restart
```

Provide the password interactively.

---

# 35. Validate the New AD Identity

After restart:

```powershell
Get-ComputerInfo |
Select-Object CsName,CsDomain,CsPartOfDomain
```

Confirm:

```text
Unique Computer Name
Correct Domain
CsPartOfDomain = True
```

The clone now has its own Active Directory computer identity.

---

# 36. Create a Unique Wazuh Agent

The clone also needs its own monitoring identity.

On the Wazuh manager:

```bash
sudo /var/ossec/bin/manage_agents
```

Create a new agent corresponding to the deployed endpoint.

Use a unique name such as:

```text
HELPDESK-TEAM01
```

Do not publish the generated authentication key.

---

# 37. Configure the Windows Agent Name

On the deployed Windows system, review:

```text
C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\ossec.conf
```

Configure the appropriate unique agent name.

Example:

```xml
<enrollment>
  <enabled>yes</enabled>
  <agent_name>HELPDESK-TEAM01</agent_name>
</enrollment>
```

---

# 38. Import the Unique Wazuh Identity

Use the Windows agent management utility:

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\manage_agents.exe"
```

Import the unique key generated for this endpoint.

The actual key should never appear in:

- GitHub
- Screenshots
- Documentation examples
- Public terminal recordings

---

# 39. Start the Agent

```powershell
Start-Service WazuhSvc
```

Verify:

```powershell
Get-Service WazuhSvc
```

---

# 40. Validate from the Manager

On the Wazuh server:

```bash
sudo /var/ossec/bin/agent_control -l
```

The deployed workstations should appear as independent agents.

Conceptually:

```text
Windows-Lab-01 -> Active
Windows-Lab-02 -> Active
Windows-Lab-03 -> Active
```

---

# 41. Enable RDP if Required

For environments using a remote access gateway:

```powershell
Set-ItemProperty `
  -Path 'HKLM:\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Terminal Server' `
  -Name 'fDenyTSConnections' `
  -Value 0
```

Enable the Windows Firewall rules:

```powershell
Enable-NetFirewallRule -DisplayGroup "Remote Desktop"
```

---

# 42. Validate RDP

```powershell
Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 3389 -State Listen
```

Then test TCP connectivity from the remote-access gateway:

```bash
nc -vz <WINDOWS_ENDPOINT_IP> 3389
```

This verifies the network path before adding another application layer such as Guacamole.

---

# 43. Validate the Entire Clone

A deployed clone should not be considered ready until all required layers have been tested.

Example:

```text
Windows boots
      |
      v
Unique hostname
      |
      v
Network works
      |
      v
Internal DNS works
      |
      v
AD membership works
      |
      v
Wazuh is Active
      |
      v
RDP works
      |
      v
Remote gateway works
```

---

# 44. Create a Known-Good Baseline

Once the deployed workstation is fully functional, create a snapshot.

Example:

```text
Clean Deployment
      |
      v
Known-Good Snapshot
      |
      v
Lab Scenario
      |
      v
Student Changes
      |
      v
Restore
```

This is what makes the environment safe to break.

---

# 45. Golden Image vs Baseline Snapshot

These serve different purposes.

### Golden Image

Used to create entirely new machines.

```text
Golden Image
     |
     v
New VM
```

### Baseline Snapshot

Used to restore an existing deployed machine.

```text
Existing VM
     |
     v
Scenario
     |
     v
Restore Snapshot
```

Both are useful in a reusable cyber lab.

---

# 46. Version the Image

As the lab evolves, the golden image will evolve too.

Example:

```text
windows-lab-v1
windows-lab-v2
windows-lab-v3
```

Document meaningful changes such as:

- Windows version
- Monitoring agent version
- New applications
- Major configuration changes
- Removed software
- Security baseline changes

---

# 47. Security Considerations

Before converting a VM into a reusable image, ask:

```text
What would every clone inherit?
```

Review the image for:

- Passwords
- API tokens
- Private keys
- SSH keys
- Enrollment secrets
- Browser sessions
- Cloud credentials
- Personal files
- Cached administrative credentials
- Certificates with private keys

A reusable image should not become a mechanism for duplicating secrets.

---

# 48. MutaSpace Reference Implementation

This workflow was used to create reusable Windows workstations for the MutaSpace Enterprise Security Lab.

During implementation, the process exposed several practical issues that are easy to miss in a diagram:

- Domain identity must be handled deliberately
- Monitoring agents can retain clone-specific identity
- Sysprep may fail because of AppX provisioning state
- Interrupted hypervisor operations can leave incomplete clones locked
- Working network connectivity does not guarantee working authentication
- Every deployed endpoint needs independent validation

Those lessons informed the repeatable workflow documented here.

---

# 49. Final Build Pattern

The complete deployment pattern is:

```text
Build Windows
      |
      v
Install Reusable Tools
      |
      v
Create Recovery Access
      |
      v
Remove Source Identity
      |
      v
Remove Reusable Secrets
      |
      v
Sysprep / Generalize
      |
      v
Protect Golden Image
      |
      v
Full Clone
      |
      v
Complete OOBE
      |
      v
Unique Hostname
      |
      v
Validate Network + DNS
      |
      v
Unique AD Identity
      |
      v
Unique Wazuh Identity
      |
      v
Configure Remote Access
      |
      v
Validate
      |
      v
Create Baseline Snapshot
```

---

# 50. Design Principle

The most important rule when cloning security lab endpoints is:

> **Standardize configuration. Regenerate identity.**

A good golden image saves time without creating duplicate identities, shared secrets, or systems that the rest of the infrastructure cannot reliably distinguish.